velocity-operator-quantum-mechanics.interpretation:
The equation $\vec{v}_i =  rac{\hbar}{m_i} \operatorname{Im}\left( rac{\vec{
abla}_i \Psi}{\Psi}ight)$ defines the velocity vector $\vec{v}_i$ of the $i$-th particle in a many-body system. Here, $\hbar$ is the reduced Planck constant, $m_i$ is the particle's mass, and $\Psi$ is the multi-particle wavefunction $\Psi(\mathbf{r}_1, ..., \mathbf{r}_N, t)$. The operator $\vec{
abla}_i$ is the gradient with respect to the coordinates of the $i$-th particle, and $\operatorname{Im}(\cdot)$ extracts the imaginary part of the complex quantity $\vec{
abla}_i \Psi / \Psi$. This equation is derived by identifying the particle velocity field with the flow of probability, $\vec{v} = \mathbf{J}/ho$, where $\mathbf{J}$ is the quantum probability current and $ho = |\Psi|^2$ is the probability density. Expressing the wavefunction in polar form, $\Psi = R e^{iS/\hbar}$, where $R$ is the real amplitude and $S$ is the real phase, reveals the physical meaning: the velocity is equivalent to $\vec{v}_i = (\vec{
abla}_i S) / m_i$. This directly connects the particle's motion to the gradient of the wavefunction's phase, which acts as the 'guiding' field.

